{{!
    @template theme_nit/core/login_panel

    NIT override of core/login_panel — what is drawn OVER the picture beside the
    account screens.

    Core fills this panel with a welcome heading, a tagline and three invented
    statistics. None of that is ours, so none of it is here. What is here is the
    two things that were previously baked into the photograph itself: the site
    logo, and a quote.

    Baked in, both went stale — a typo meant re-exporting an image, and the logo
    stayed whatever it was on the day the picture was made. Drawn here instead,
    the logo IS the site's logo (the navbar's, resolved in PHP so there is one
    logo on the site and not two) and the quote is a field an administrator edits
    on the gallery page's "Log-in & sign-up" tab.

    Both are optional: with no logo configured and no quote written, this renders
    an empty panel and the picture is the whole of it — which is what it was
    before.

    Kept as a theme override rather than an edit to theme/boost's copy, so Moodle
    upgrades never conflict with, or silently revert, this.

    Context variables required for this template:
    * nitpanel.haslogo - whether there is a logo to draw
    * nitpanel.logourl - its URL
    * nitpanel.sitename - the site's full name, for the logo's alt text
    * nitpanel.hasquote - whether a quote has been written
    * nitpanel.quote - the quote, already format_string()'d
    * nitpanel.hasauthor - whether an attribution line was written with it
    * nitpanel.author - that line, already format_string()'d

    All of it is built by theme_nit_auth_panel_content(), called from
    theme/nit/layout/login.php — a partial cannot fetch its own data.

    Example context (json):
    {
        "nitpanel": {
            "haslogo": true,
            "logourl": "https://example.com/pluginfile.php/1/core_admin/logocompact/logo.png",
            "sitename": "EAAC Learning Platform",
            "hasquote": true,
            "quote": "The beautiful thing about learning is that no one can take it away from you.",
            "hasauthor": true,
            "author": "Brian Herbert &middot; Educational Leader"
        }
    }
}}
<div class="nit-authpanel">
    <div class="nit-authpanel-brand">
        {{#nitpanel.haslogo}}
            <img src="{{nitpanel.logourl}}" alt="{{nitpanel.sitename}}" class="nit-authpanel-logo">
        {{/nitpanel.haslogo}}
    </div>

    {{#nitpanel.hasquote}}
        {{!
            A figure, not a bare div: the attribution is a caption of the quote and
            not a second sentence of it, which is the distinction figcaption exists
            to make and the one a screen reader needs to hear.
        }}
        <figure class="nit-authpanel-quote">
            <blockquote class="nit-authpanel-quote-text">{{{nitpanel.quote}}}</blockquote>
            {{#nitpanel.hasauthor}}
                <figcaption class="nit-authpanel-quote-author">
                    {{! Decoration standing in for the em dash - not read out. }}
                    <span class="nit-authpanel-rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    <span>{{{nitpanel.author}}}</span>
                </figcaption>
            {{/nitpanel.hasauthor}}
        </figure>
    {{/nitpanel.hasquote}}
</div>
